 # 3DGS-MCMC
 
-Also called: MCMC-based Gaussian Lifecycle, Probabilistic Densification
+Also called: MCMC-based Gaussian Lifecycle
 
 ## Overview
-3DGS-MCMC replaces the noisy positional gradient magnitude heuristic used in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting with a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) framework to guide the spawning and pruning of Gaussian primitives [S1, unofficial]. By treating the Gaussian cloud as samples from an underlying probability distribution, it explores regions with unknown gradients more effectively, yielding denser, more efficient, and artifact-free scene representations [S1, unofficial].
+3DGS-MCMC replaces the noisy positional gradient magnitude heuristics of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting with a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) framework to guide the life cycle (spawn and prune) of Gaussian primitives [S1, unofficial][S2]. By treating the Gaussian cloud as samples from an underlying probability distribution, it dynamically adds and removes Gaussians based on rendering error and proposal probabilities, yielding more efficient, artifact-free clouds with sharper details compared to the original densification process [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- Current set of Gaussians $\mathcal{G}$, each with mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$, opacity $\alpha$, and spherical harmonics coefficients.
-- Rendering loss $\mathcal{L}$ computed for the current frame or batch.
-- Proposal distribution $q(\cdot|\cdot)$ for generating candidate new Gaussian positions.
+*   Current set of Gaussians $\mathcal{G} = \{g_1, \dots, g_N\}$, where each $g_k$ has mean $\mu_k$, covariance $\Sigma_k$, opacity $\alpha_k$, and spherical harmonic coefficients $\mathbf{c}_k$ [Parent].
+*   Current training loss $\mathcal{L}$ computed from rendered images.
+*   Proposal distribution $q(\cdot|\cdot)$ for generating candidate Gaussian positions.
 
 **Outputs:**
-- Updated set of Gaussians $\mathcal{G}'$ with modified population based on MCMC acceptance/rejection.
+*   Updated set of Gaussians $\mathcal{G}'$ after MCMC sampling steps.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- Gaussian population $\mathcal{G}$: List or array of Gaussian objects. Each object contains fields for mean $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathbb{R}^3$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$ (or rotation/scale parameters), opacity $\alpha \in [0, 1]$, and feature vectors.
-- Loss buffer: Stores $\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_k)$ for each Gaussian $k$ or per-pixel loss contributions.
+*   The Gaussian cloud is maintained as a list or array of Gaussian objects.
+*   Each Gaussian object stores: $\mu \in \mathbb{R}^3$, $\Sigma \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$ (or parameters $s, q$), $\alpha \in [0, 1]$, $\mathbf{c}$ (SH coefficients).
+*   Buffer management is required to handle dynamic addition/removal, typically via flag-based marking or compaction [Parent].
 
